<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jingle Bells</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first performed 15 September <a href="/years/1857">1857</a>; copyrighted 16 September <a href="/years/1857">1857</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Lord Pierpont copyrighted the song as &quot;The One Horse Open Sleigh&quot; on 16 September 1857, credited as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Chorus written and composed by J. Pierpont&quot;, and dedicated it to John P. Ordway, organiser of a troupe called Ordway's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolians</a>. It was first performed the day before, on 15 September 1857, at Ordway Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, by the blackface minstrel performer Johnny Pell.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>It is not known where or when James Lord Pierpont first composed the song that became Jingle Bells. A plaque at 19 High Street in Medford Square, Massachusetts, claims that Pierpont wrote it there in 1850, at what was then the Simpson Tavern. The researcher Kyna Hamill proposes instead that he composed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, before moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> in the autumn of 1857.</p><p>By the time of its release Pierpont was organist and music director of the Unitarian church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where his brother Rev. John Pierpont Jr. was minister. In August 1857 he married Eliza Jane Purse, daughter of the mayor of Savannah, and he never returned north. The song belonged to the then-popular genre of sleighing songs, and Hamill argues that its lyrics closely resemble lines from many others and that Pierpont's need for money led him to release it as a financial enterprise. It was possibly intended as a drinking song and had no original connection with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The song was republished in 1859 by Oliver Ditson and Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> as Jingle Bells; or, The One Horse Open Sleigh. The music historian James Fuld notes that jingle in the title is apparently an imperative verb, though jingle bells is commonly read as a kind of bell. In pre-automobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> horses' harnesses carried bells to avoid collisions at blind intersections, since a sleigh in snow makes almost no noise, and the rhythm of the tune seems to imitate a trotting horse.</p><p>The first two stanzas and the chorus of the 1857 lyrics differed slightly from the modern words, and it is unknown who changed them. The 1857 version also had a substantially different chorus. The simpler modern refrain has an unknown origin but dates back at least to 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song became associated with winter and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> in the 1860s and 1870s. The banjoist Will Lyle recorded it on an Edison cylinder on 30 October 1889, believed to be the first Christmas record, though no copy survives. The earliest surviving vocal recording, by the Edison Male Quartette, dates from 1898. In 1943 Bing Crosby and the Andrews Sisters sold over a million copies of their recording. On 16 December 1965 the Gemini 6 astronauts Tom Stafford and Wally Schirra broadcast it from space as a prank, with a smuggled harmonica and sleigh bells. The tune is also used for the French song Vive le vent and for several German songs, none with related lyrics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jingle Bells” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oliver Ditson and Company of Boston republished the song in 1859 under a new title, &quot;<b>Jingle Bells</b>; or, The One Horse Open Sleigh&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jingle-bells">Jingle Bells</a></li></ul></section>
